4752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435048 1079 #uuid "37f01642-3348-59ae-92b2-38fd784b6dae" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435049 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435044 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435049 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435049 1077 "field-1" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435049 1078 #edb/ref 17592186435044 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435049 1079 #uuid "4b1af39d-b0cd-5234-a9e5-f7a9cf9d0cfe" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435050 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435031 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435050 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435032 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435050 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435036 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435050 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435040 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435050 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435045 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435050 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435050 1077 "selection" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435050 1078 #edb/ref 17592186435036 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435050 1079 #uuid "8d2fe7da-7162-512f-acab-d05c2d961b5b" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435051 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435038 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435051 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435051 1077 "field-1" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435051 1078 #edb/ref 17592186435038 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435051 1079 #uuid "ad249e65-93ce-5e24-9e7f-3d95d55441e3" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435052 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435023 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435052 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435026 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435052 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435030 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435052 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435034 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435052 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435039 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435052 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435052 1077 "selection" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435052 1078 #edb/ref 17592186435039 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435052 1079 #uuid "e3b7845b-8ad3-57c0-a6ae-28437acfe3a6" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435053 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435047 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435053 1069 "q-141235" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435053 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435053 1071 "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nI. $(6-{x}^{3})-({x}^{2}+4)$\n\nII. $(3{x}^{3}+x)\\cdot (2{x}^{4}+4)$\n\nIII. $(1-3{x}^{3})÷(3x)$" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435053 1072 #uuid "17b4b8a5-2a1a-5b8f-9d66-afed9881e7a3" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435053 1074 "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a difference between two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Expression II gives a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-3{x}^{3}}{3x} & =\\frac{1}{3x}-\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{3x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3x}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435054 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435050 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435054 1069 "q-111386" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435054 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435054 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $4$ and $8,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P+Q$ is a polynomial of degree $8$\n\nII. $P\\cdot Q$ is a polynomial of degree $32$\n\nIII. $\\frac{Q}{P}$ is a polynomial of degree $4$" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435054 1072 #uuid "2357828d-1c68-5412-b5ab-9d7c9a1f8663" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435054 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{4}$ and ${x}^{8},$ respectively. Since there is no ${x}^{8}$ term in $P$ that could possibly cancel the ${x}^{8}$ term in $Q,$ the leading term in $P+Q,$ still contains ${x}^{8}.$ Therefore, the degree of $P+Q$ must always be $8.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since the highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{4}$ and ${x}^{8},$ respectively, the highest-degree term in $P\\cdot Q$ must contain ${x}^{4}\\cdot {x}^{8}={x}^{4+8}={x}^{12},$ meaning that $P\\cdot Q$ has degree $12.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{4}$ and $Q={x}^{8}+1.$ Then, $\\frac{Q}{P}=\\frac{{x}^{8}+1}{{x}^{4}}={x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}},$ which is not a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435055 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435052 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435055 1069 "q-111389" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435055 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435055 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P+Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $1$\n\nII. $P-Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $2$\n\nIII. $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is always a polynomial" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435055 1072 #uuid "44852747-843f-5a59-aaa4-c3f273f2d780" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435055 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. For example, suppose that $P={x}^{3}+x$ and $Q=-{x}^{3}.$ Then, $P+Q={x}^{3}+x-{x}^{3}=x,$ which is a polynomial of degree $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. For example, suppose that $P={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}$ and $Q={x}^{3}.$ Then, $P-Q={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}={x}^{2},$ which is a polynomial of degree $2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P=1+{x}^{3}$ and $Q={x}^{3}.$ Then, $\\frac{P}{Q}=\\frac{1+{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+1,$ which is not a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435056 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435048 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435056 1069 "q-277912" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435056 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435056 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $7$, the **smallest possible** degree of $P+Q$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435056 1072 #uuid "55ca9374-3367-5974-bb86-994acf801013" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435056 1074 "The smallest possible degree of $P-Q$ is $0.$\n\nFor example, suppose $P=9{x}^{7}$ and $Q=-9{x}^{7}-4.$ Then $P+Q=-4,$ which is a polynomial of degree $0.$" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435057 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435049 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435057 1069 "q-277897" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435057 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435057 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $7$ and $8$, respectively,  the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435057 1072 #uuid "68e7c276-c403-5f7f-bb19-14fc56277444" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435057 1074 "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $7,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{7}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $8,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{8}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{7}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{8}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{7}\\cdot {x}^{8}={x}^{7+8}={x}^{15}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $15.$" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435058 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435051 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435058 1069 "q-269923" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435058 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435058 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $12$,  the highest possible degree of $P+Q$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435058 1072 #uuid "a7a2bab2-b83f-574b-87f1-a6024a7e2d15" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435058 1074 "The highest possible degree of $P+Q$ is $12.$\n\nFor example, suppose $P={x}^{12}$ and $Q={x}^{12}.$ Then $P+Q=2{x}^{12}.$ \n\nSince neither $P$ nor $Q$ contain terms of order higher than ${x}^{12},$ $P+Q$ can't contain terms of order higher than ${x}^{12}.$" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435059 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435046 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435059 1069 "q-141256" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435059 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435059 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $9$, respectively, what is the degree of $P\\cdot Q?$" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435059 1072 #uuid "c8719458-2bd9-576f-821e-1af82c528d6c" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true] [17592186435059 1074 "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $3,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{3}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $9,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{9}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{3}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{9}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{9}={x}^{3+9}={x}^{12}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $12.$" 13194139534752 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-045d0a65-b7d8-5bfb-9b43-906b5283f84a" 17592186435023 "answer-0ea4ef8b-f443-5c71-a66d-968c904fd206" 17592186435024 "answer-13fc7668-35a7-52b2-8d6b-1ef66cb2dee3" 17592186435025 "answer-14893a56-3116-5bfd-b0d1-990957ad1998" 17592186435026 "answer-2219b244-05db-51b1-afdc-21618a8ff9db" 17592186435027 "answer-22efd206-8495-5c5b-82c3-8b22f6169935" 17592186435028 "answer-24ca3551-0275-501a-8224-d0c29cf4662c" 17592186435029 "answer-2a8cb1de-ccb8-5910-9ec5-4dba43048b8b" 17592186435030 "answer-5582da46-e860-5de6-9a80-bf70a2d5dbc2" 17592186435031 "answer-5eba3768-372e-57d2-9f12-5041b396542f" 17592186435032 "answer-5f573ec0-e420-5d5a-bf15-12b9f19228c1" 17592186435033 "answer-79f71a19-7850-565d-91b5-d77d8197d35f" 17592186435034 "answer-8782acb7-46a5-5731-bad8-7ee07d94b5ed" 17592186435035 "answer-8831a439-f885-54bc-913a-726e2783d364" 17592186435036 "answer-972d63cb-deb1-504d-983c-3afd89efb163" 17592186435037 "answer-b0cbcf02-f7a2-529e-aa13-2e2ba426321d" 17592186435038 "answer-b4dd91c3-ef1a-5e41-ab0d-dd262ea8b55c" 17592186435039 "answer-d08c9c2d-c678-54bf-8b79-6e361af4291c" 17592186435040 "answer-db22347e-ef69-54bb-bdf3-e3c186808327" 17592186435041 "answer-dfa2f6a3-912d-5f28-9899-584bb9cb2f53" 17592186435042 "answer-e1cafff7-1db2-590c-918b-31d40bb1ec62" 17592186435043 "answer-f727d8b3-fde4-50e2-8609-459d27c079b9" 17592186435044 "answer-f7c7fe9a-33a1-5066-a984-2b33bf4beec0" 17592186435045 "answer-field-18301aa5-8122-537f-afa8-0033da27bf6e" 17592186435046 "answer-field-3642f741-bfcb-537b-8055-8377c2f4a23c" 17592186435047 "answer-field-37f01642-3348-59ae-92b2-38fd784b6dae" 17592186435048 "answer-field-4b1af39d-b0cd-5234-a9e5-f7a9cf9d0cfe" 17592186435049 "answer-field-8d2fe7da-7162-512f-acab-d05c2d961b5b" 175921